            <div xml:id="d261">

                <pb facs="0375" n="280" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg280"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/13692: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Second Secretary of Embassy in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Atcheson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-09-05T12:00:00+08:00">September 5,
                        1936—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received September 5—10:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>260. My 255, September 1, 10 a.m.<anchor n="93" xml:id="d261-f1"/></p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>According to the press, Wang Chung Hui did not accompany the Peace Mission
                        to Nanning on account of illness and the mission’s journey was delayed by
                        weather. Reuter at Canton reports September 4 that the Peace Conference at
                        Nanning has come to a deadlock because Li Tsung Jen and Pai Chung Hsi insist
                        that, (1) they remain at Kwangsi as Pacification Commissioner and
                        Vice-Commissioner and, (2) Li Chi Sen be appointed Kwangsi Chairman. It is
                        stated that Chiang Kai Shek’s representatives were to return to Canton
                        yesterday.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>We have so far been unable to obtain reliable information concerning the
                        Nanning negotiations or the plans of the Government if the deadlock
                        continues. As opposed to the statement of a Foreign Office official under
                        cover in paragraph 3 of the telegram under reference, I am informed by a
                        senior officer of the Executive Yuan that the Government will in no
                        circumstances, other than an attack by Japanese, launch into contests,
                        although its position is legally and <pb facs="0376" n="281" xml:id="pg281"
                        /> ethically correct, because it fears that it cannot rely upon Han Fu Chu,
                        Sung Che Yuan, Feng Yu Hsiang, Liu Hsiang and possibly others to maintain an
                        attitude of support.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Sent to the Department, Peiping and Canton.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Atcheson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
